1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,940
Okay, so I'm around at least.

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:07,720
If you've got questions about the museum, happy to take them.

3
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:09,440
All right.

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:14,120
So, where were we?

5
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:16,240
Yes. So we've been going over the law hazard,

6
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:19,400
and I was mentioning the great neglected topic

7
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:20,820
of voluntary punishment.

8
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:24,080
Like why can't you write a contract

9
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,260
where you specify how you'll be punished

10
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:27,340
if you break the contract?

11
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,920
And why isn't a contract like that enforceable

12
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:31,400
under Anglo-American law,

13
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,220
which traditionally does not fit, right?

14
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:36,240
And if you read the legal statements,

15
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,040
it's even more confusing,

16
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:40,560
because you'll just get some

17
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:42,420
of the most question-begging comments like,

18
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,480
well, it is not the purpose of the law to punish.

19
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:50,160
This is the punishment that is least objectionable.

20
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:51,900
This is punishment someone signed a contract

21
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:53,860
and said they wanted to accept the punishment.

22
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:57,220
And of course, basic compensating differentials,

23
00:00:57,220 --> 00:01:00,600
if a contract is very slanted against you, what does that do

24
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:01,860
to the price you receive?

25
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:04,620
Yeah, you get a better deal.

26
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:06,900
So, if you say, you catch me playing Tetris,

27
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:08,520
you can fine me $5,000.

28
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,080
That makes it easier for you to get a high-paying job,

29
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:12,900
because then people feel comfortable

30
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:15,480
that they are not going to actually be playing a Tet,

31
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:16,900
paying you to play Tetris all day.

32
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:20,340
And as I said, Robin Henson has a chapter

33
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:21,700
in his dissertation on this topic.

34
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:24,320
I haven't heard anyone else doing anything more with this,

35
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:27,780
even though it seems like an obvious and great idea.

36
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:31,780
So, one day, someone in this class will take the idea

37
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:34,280
and run with it, and this could be the year for this.

38
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:36,180
All right.

39
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:39,240
So, moving on.

40
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:41,180
Adverse selection.

41
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:43,300
All right, so we've already done moral hazard.

42
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:44,380
Now, adverse selection.

43
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:46,760
All right, this is a much more complex form

44
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:47,960
of asymmetric information.

45
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:50,180
Moral hazard is really very simple, right?

46
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:52,160
It's just the idea, once you have insurance,

47
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:53,740
then you're going to do, you're going to take more risks,

48
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:54,440
do more on the way.

49
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:56,880
If someone else is picking up the bill, then you're going

50
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:57,940
to do more bad things.

51
00:01:58,480 --> 00:01:59,700
Like, no big surprise there.

52
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:00,720
All right.

53
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:05,280
But adverse selection is a little bit more complicated,

54
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:10,740
and not really obvious at first to people what's going on.

55
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:12,460
All right, so the basic idea

56
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:15,540
of adverse selection, though, is you know what you are.

57
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:17,500
You know the real you.

58
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,280
But the world, however, does not know the real you.

59
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:24,360
The world has to judge you based upon your superficial

60
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:25,280
characteristics.

61
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:26,660
What you look like.

62
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:28,160
What you seem like on the surface.

63
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:31,100
Which, of course, could be literal physical appearance.

64
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:34,760
Like if I had a spider web face tattoo.

65
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:38,040
That's as superficial a trait as you can get.

66
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:39,780
And yet, you would judge me for it.

67
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:42,640
Right? Or I remember once my, who was it?

68
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:46,200
Once my daughter had lice, so I just decided to shave my head.

69
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:48,560
And I'm walking around saying, wow, I look really tough now.

70
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,920
Like Walter White.

71
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:52,540
I'm walking around with a bald head.

72
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:54,620
Someone, people should be scared to mess with me.

73
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:57,700
And I look in the mirror, like I actually am a little scary now.

74
00:02:57,700 --> 00:02:59,900
Whereas normally, not scary at all.

75
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:03,140
Right? And what's so scary about having a shaved head?

76
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:04,380
I don't know.

77
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:08,480
But, look, but simple story is that on average,

78
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:11,480
the guys with the shaved heads are, in fact, scarier.

79
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:13,360
So if you happen to shave your head

80
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:16,560
for a totally unrelated reason, namely lice paranoia,

81
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:21,020
like how can I be absolutely sure there isn't lice in my hair?

82
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:24,560
I can't be, oh, every little feel,

83
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:26,760
if you feel it in your scalp, oh, there could be lice.

84
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:28,060
Or just take it off.

85
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:29,360
Shave all the hair off.

86
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:32,440
And now, I'm not trying to be scary.

87
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:34,340
I'm just trying to get rid of the lice.

88
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:36,880
But people walk near the street and they judge me

89
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:39,880
as a scary guy, at least on average.

90
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:42,040
They will think I'm scarier than I would normally anyway.

91
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:44,300
So, you know, yes?

92
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:46,760
Maybe they think you're scary because you might have lice.

93
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:52,200
It's not normally, it's disgusting more than scary.

94
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:52,880
But, yeah.

95
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:53,840
All right.

96
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:56,360
So, anyway, it could be a totally superficial trait.

97
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:59,400
But it could also be superficial as in your resume.

98
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:01,980
Your resume isn't the real you, is it?

99
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:05,080
There's more to you than what you can put on one piece

100
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:08,460
of paper about your signature strengths and so on.

101
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:11,380
And yet, if someone's trying to decide to interview you,

102
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:13,920
they're likely to do it just based purely upon the basis

103
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:16,640
of what's on this piece of paper, right?

104
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:18,220
Or when you're coming up for tenure.

105
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:21,480
Yes, there are people who actually count the lines

106
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:23,300
on the VITA, right?

107
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:26,200
At least, normally, they only count the publication lines.

108
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:29,500
And so, but, you know, other people might count even more.

109
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:31,140
So, you know, he's got 10 publications.

110
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:32,100
That's good enough here.

111
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:33,100
You're like, what?

112
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:34,660
They don't weight them by quality?

113
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:37,040
Well, the higher the quality of the place

114
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:38,300
that you're being evaluated at,

115
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:39,500
the more weight you put on quality.

116
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:42,240
There are plenty of places where publications

117
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:45,060
and publication, hence the joke about getting published

118
00:04:45,060 --> 00:04:49,820
in capital A, capital R, refereed, capital J journal,

119
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:51,860
A referee journal, right?

120
00:04:51,860 --> 00:04:54,580
So, where all you need is a publication somewhere or other,

121
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:56,360
and that can count, right?

122
00:04:56,360 --> 00:04:58,040
And this is where someone is being very superficial.

123
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:00,900
Like, well, I don't know the quality of the journals

124
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:02,300
or the quality of the articles.

125
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:04,820
I'm just going to hand out dream jobs for life based

126
00:05:04,820 --> 00:05:06,420
upon whether you got 10 publications

127
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:07,160
in something or other.

128
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:08,160
All right?

129
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:10,140
So, that's another way that people might do it.

130
00:05:10,140 --> 00:05:13,940
Okay. So, there's lots of different kind

131
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:16,680
of superficialities, physical appearance,

132
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:19,400
what's written on a page, you know, your car,

133
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:22,500
the clothes you're wearing, you know, like your family,

134
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,280
your family name, your race, your gender.

135
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:27,740
There's so many different ways that someone could just take two

136
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:30,040
seconds to look at you and then say, yeah, all right,

137
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:31,340
like a three out of 10.

138
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:32,220
All right?

139
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:32,840
Very simple.

140
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:34,140
That's all I need to know.

141
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:35,880
At least that's all I'm going to bother learning.

142
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:38,020
Okay. All right.

143
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:40,780
Now, so you know, however, what you're really like.

144
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:42,400
You have your selection model.

145
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:44,740
Other people are going to treat you based upon the average

146
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:47,420
characteristics of people who superficially resemble you.

147
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:49,340
Now, what does this mean?

148
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:51,980
Well, suppose that you are above average.

149
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,960
You're above average for people who look like you.

150
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:59,420
Then you might decide that there's no point participating

151
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:02,780
because you get treated like you're average even though you're

152
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:03,640
better than average.

153
00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:05,280
Let's see if I can get that to fall.

154
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:06,560
There. That's good.

155
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:07,560
All right.

156
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:09,720
So you get treated like you are average when, in fact,

157
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:10,620
you're better than average.

158
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:13,520
So if you have any options of whether you want to participate

159
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:16,360
in something, above average people naturally want

160
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:17,060
to drop out.

161
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:19,580
Right? At least they are more inclined to drop out.

162
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:21,920
Whereas below average people, on the other hand, say, hey,

163
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:22,380
this is great.

164
00:06:22,380 --> 00:06:23,940
I love being treated like I'm average

165
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:25,360
because I'm not average.

166
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:26,500
I'm terrible, actually.

167
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,220
But I can blend in.

168
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:29,780
I can just blend in.

169
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:35,440
Right? So if you are, say, what would it be?

170
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:38,780
Yeah. So suppose that you look like me.

171
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:39,580
Right? All right.

172
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:40,900
So look at this here.

173
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:45,780
But suppose that actually you are even more socially inept

174
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:47,680
than someone who looks like me would normally be.

175
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,240
In that case, you say, isn't it great I get judged

176
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:53,460
as just being average because I'm not average, actually.

177
00:06:53,820 --> 00:06:57,560
On the other hand, if you are the unusually socially adept guy

178
00:06:57,560 --> 00:07:00,240
who wears a Mutants of Mastermind shirt, then,

179
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:02,980
those are the cases where you say, well,

180
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:04,500
maybe I don't want to participate

181
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,860
because I get treated like scum.

182
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:08,880
Right? So that's the idea.

183
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:09,940
All right.

184
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:11,220
Now, here's the problem.

185
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:13,680
If enough above average people think this way,

186
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,060
then the average goes down.

187
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:17,780
And when the average goes down,

188
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:19,440
then more people may want to drop out.

189
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:21,440
And then when more people, and who wants to drop out again?

190
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:23,980
The people that, the best of the remaining people.

191
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:25,960
And then the best of the remaining people.

192
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:27,080
And the best of the remaining people.

193
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:28,660
As I think I said last class,

194
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:30,900
imagine if I said I failed the bottom half of the class.

195
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:33,340
Well, if you know where you're going to be,

196
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:34,520
then the bottom half drops.

197
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:37,220
But then I said, well, I didn't say I would fail the bottom half

198
00:07:37,220 --> 00:07:38,460
of the initial class.

199
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:41,000
I said I'd fail the bottom half of the class,

200
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:42,820
which is defined as people who are enrolled.

201
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:44,600
And I said, all right, now I've got to worry

202
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:48,000
from the bottom half of the top half, the original top half.

203
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:49,360
And that group drops.

204
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:51,780
And then the bottom half of the remainder may drop.

205
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:52,300
And so on.

206
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:53,260
Same idea.

207
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:54,200
All right.

208
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:57,200
Now, there's a classic example, which is surprising.

209
00:07:57,200 --> 00:07:58,220
Oh, can you close the door back there?

210
00:07:58,220 --> 00:08:03,100
So there's a classic example, which has also been

211
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:04,100
done experimentally.

212
00:08:04,100 --> 00:08:06,720
And again, it's a very similar model with a result

213
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:09,000
that is extremely surprising to almost everyone

214
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,280
who hasn't actually worked through the model in detail.

215
00:08:12,060 --> 00:08:12,320
All right.

216
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:13,300
And here's the idea.

217
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:17,500
Suppose that there are companies with true values

218
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:19,880
that are uniformly distributed between 0 and 100.

219
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:20,920
All right.

220
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:24,880
But, key point, every company is worth 50% more

221
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:26,060
in the hands of the buyer.

222
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:28,300
So there's sellers who have these companies.

223
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:29,900
But if you can find a buyer,

224
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:32,460
the buyer will actually be better at managing the company.

225
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:34,260
And you just inherited this company, say.

226
00:08:34,260 --> 00:08:35,980
You inherited it, but you know how good it is.

227
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:37,020
That's key.

228
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:38,940
So you sell it to someone else who knows what they're doing,

229
00:08:38,940 --> 00:08:41,100
it'll be worth 50% more in their hands than in your hands.

230
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:42,360
All right.

231
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,620
So, now all that buyers know, let's assume,

232
00:08:46,820 --> 00:08:49,100
is the average value of companies that are being sold.

233
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:51,640
They don't know whether your company is good or not.

234
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:53,500
All they know is what's average.

235
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:54,340
All right.

236
00:08:54,340 --> 00:08:57,920
So, of course, the average value of these companies,

237
00:08:57,920 --> 00:08:59,800
since it's a uniform distribution, is 50.

238
00:09:00,460 --> 00:09:04,800
Right? And a normal reaction people have is, well,

239
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:07,160
why don't we, is that, why don't we just bid 50?

240
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:08,640
Let's just bid 50.

241
00:09:09,260 --> 00:09:14,720
And then the idea is if we bid 50, then 50 plus 50% is 75.

242
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:17,520
So then we're going to go and get, and we have a profit of 25.

243
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:19,080
But that's wrong.

244
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:20,520
That's so wrong.

245
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:25,320
That's so wrong, because if you bid 50, which companies will not be sold?

246
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:27,080
The ones that are worth more than 50.

247
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:30,980
Yes. None of these companies are on the market when you offer 50.

248
00:09:31,560 --> 00:09:36,060
And, therefore, the companies that are going to be sold will have an average value of 25.

249
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:45,060
And 50% more than 25 is 37.5.

250
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:50,520
And since you bid 50, and on average you only, you get a company worth 37.5 to you,

251
00:09:50,740 --> 00:09:52,200
you have lost 12.5.

252
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:53,520
All right.

253
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:54,460
So that didn't work out.

254
00:09:54,900 --> 00:09:57,080
And you might say, well, why don't we go and up our bid?

255
00:09:57,080 --> 00:09:58,360
How about we go and bid 80?

256
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:02,380
Well, if you bid 80, then you do have more good companies that are in the mix.

257
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:05,980
But still, the top companies don't, are not going to be involved.

258
00:10:06,820 --> 00:10:10,200
And, therefore, you're going to have all companies that are worth between zero

259
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:12,240
and 80 to the sellers.

260
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:16,220
And the average of that is, of course, 40.

