00:32:19,480 --> 00:32:22,440
Sure, but given that's legal right now,

314
00:32:22,440 --> 00:32:25,400
so it's very reasonable to say that insurance companies would

315
00:32:25,400 --> 00:32:27,600
try to compensate for the problems.

316
00:32:27,600 --> 00:32:30,120
But still, it seems unlikely they'd

317
00:32:30,120 --> 00:32:32,440
be able to compensate as well because insurance companies

318
00:32:32,440 --> 00:32:33,240
can't jail anybody.

319
00:32:33,240 --> 00:32:35,400
Remember what I said, you can't contractually

320
00:32:35,400 --> 00:32:37,120
agree to private punishments.

321
00:32:37,120 --> 00:32:39,840
So yeah, you can imagine saying, I signed the insurance contract,

322
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:41,640
and if I'm found guilty of insurance fraud,

323
00:32:41,640 --> 00:32:43,960
you can go and take everything I own.

324
00:32:43,960 --> 00:32:45,840
Just based on credit.

325
00:32:45,840 --> 00:32:50,160
Yeah, so but yes, but if the harsher penalties

326
00:32:50,160 --> 00:32:52,520
are unavailable to private organizations,

327
00:32:52,520 --> 00:32:54,200
but government has them, if government

328
00:32:54,200 --> 00:32:56,200
stops providing them, then probably

329
00:32:56,200 --> 00:32:59,480
it's going to be hard for the other alternatives

330
00:32:59,480 --> 00:33:00,280
to be as effective.

331
00:33:00,280 --> 00:33:02,880
Although, maybe they would be.

332
00:33:02,880 --> 00:33:07,040
So adverse selection is a very common rationale

333
00:33:07,040 --> 00:33:10,080
economists have for a law requiring everybody

334
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:12,040
to buy insurance, which of course

335
00:33:12,040 --> 00:33:15,120
would be for car insurance or for health insurance.

336
00:33:15,120 --> 00:33:17,560
Anytime the law says you have to have insurance,

337
00:33:17,560 --> 00:33:20,880
there is a very simple story behind it

338
00:33:20,880 --> 00:33:24,640
that economists have, which is adverse selection.

339
00:33:24,640 --> 00:33:26,680
So again, in this case, imagine there

340
00:33:26,680 --> 00:33:28,520
were laws saying you must sell your company.

341
00:33:28,520 --> 00:33:30,920
You can't keep your company.

342
00:33:30,920 --> 00:33:33,880
So in that case, everyone sells their company,

343
00:33:33,880 --> 00:33:36,120
even though they know that the companies are actually

344
00:33:36,120 --> 00:33:39,120
worth more than what they're selling, or some of them.

345
00:33:39,120 --> 00:33:40,840
So in that case, all are sold.

346
00:33:40,840 --> 00:33:42,680
So all companies from 0 to 100 are sold,

347
00:33:42,680 --> 00:33:45,360
and the average value to the buyers is 50.

348
00:33:45,360 --> 00:33:47,520
And then sellers will be happy to buy,

349
00:33:47,520 --> 00:33:49,440
because they know that on average,

350
00:33:49,440 --> 00:33:55,320
they get a company that's worth 50 to the seller, 75 to them.

351
00:33:55,320 --> 00:33:57,280
And again, the interesting thing is

352
00:33:57,280 --> 00:34:01,440
that a lot of the sellers gain, although of course not all.

353
00:34:01,440 --> 00:34:04,560
Who are the sellers who lose as a result of the mandate?

354
00:34:04,560 --> 00:34:06,160
Sellers whose company is going to win.

355
00:34:06,160 --> 00:34:08,160
What's that?

356
00:34:08,160 --> 00:34:09,160
Well, let's see.

357
00:34:09,160 --> 00:34:11,840
Well, so let's see.

358
00:34:11,840 --> 00:34:12,480
Yes, yes, yes.

359
00:34:12,480 --> 00:34:14,520
So the story that I told, yes, then exactly.

360
00:34:14,520 --> 00:34:16,720
So all these people lose.

361
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:19,240
So then how is there an efficiency gain?

362
00:34:19,240 --> 00:34:21,640
Well, the answer is when you add up the gains to all

363
00:34:21,640 --> 00:34:26,080
of these people, plus the profits to the buyers,

364
00:34:26,080 --> 00:34:28,480
it exceeds the losses to the sellers.

365
00:34:28,480 --> 00:34:32,160
And that is our definition of which kind of efficiency?

366
00:34:32,160 --> 00:34:32,800
Gallery Hicks.

367
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:33,840
Gallery Hicks.

368
00:34:33,840 --> 00:34:35,560
Right, yes.

369
00:34:35,560 --> 00:34:39,240
If it's mandatory to sell at the price offered,

370
00:34:39,240 --> 00:34:42,720
why wouldn't the buyers offer a lower price?

371
00:34:42,720 --> 00:34:44,220
So there could be competition there.

372
00:34:44,220 --> 00:34:47,720
So basically, the buyers bid for a car.

373
00:34:47,720 --> 00:34:50,200
And with perfect competition, you

374
00:34:50,200 --> 00:34:52,600
think they would actually bid the price up to $75.

375
00:34:52,600 --> 00:34:54,040
And then actually even more gain.

376
00:34:54,040 --> 00:34:57,560
In that case, all of these people gain.

377
00:34:57,560 --> 00:35:00,560
On average, the buyers have no profit

378
00:35:00,560 --> 00:35:03,600
because they're going to be paying $75 for companies that

379
00:35:03,600 --> 00:35:05,520
are worth $175 to them.

380
00:35:05,520 --> 00:35:09,440
And then everything comes out of the hides of these people.

381
00:35:09,440 --> 00:35:11,160
But they don't even lose that much.

382
00:35:11,160 --> 00:35:14,920
That's the amazing thing.

383
00:35:14,920 --> 00:35:21,600
So adverse selection, standard justification for a mandate.

384
00:35:21,600 --> 00:35:24,240
Now, if you're wondering, what is

385
00:35:24,240 --> 00:35:28,560
the deal with all the other parts of things like Obamacare?

386
00:35:28,560 --> 00:35:30,120
There's a great Paul Krugman column

387
00:35:30,120 --> 00:35:31,640
where he goes and tries to explain it.

388
00:35:31,640 --> 00:35:34,280
Because what, of course, is the part of Obamacare

389
00:35:34,280 --> 00:35:35,960
that almost everybody hates?

390
00:35:35,960 --> 00:35:36,720
The mandate.

391
00:35:36,720 --> 00:35:38,760
The mandate, which is the only part actually

392
00:35:38,760 --> 00:35:40,800
justified by adverse selection.

393
00:35:40,800 --> 00:35:42,080
So the part that actually has anything

394
00:35:42,080 --> 00:35:44,040
to do with the market failure that we're talking about

395
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:45,800
is the part that most people hate.

396
00:35:45,800 --> 00:35:47,800
So there's actually a very good survey

397
00:35:47,800 --> 00:35:50,040
that breaks up the different components of Obamacare

398
00:35:50,040 --> 00:35:51,320
that are the popularity.

399
00:35:51,320 --> 00:35:54,680
And I think only like 30% of Americans support the mandate.

400
00:35:54,680 --> 00:35:58,120
All right, now, what are the other parts?

401
00:35:58,120 --> 00:36:00,520
So the parts that people like.

402
00:36:00,520 --> 00:36:04,160
All right, well, there's the ban on pre-existing conditions

403
00:36:04,160 --> 00:36:07,160
clauses.

404
00:36:07,160 --> 00:36:10,480
All right, and remember, so pre-existing conditions

405
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:14,200
clause, this is a clause where an insurance company says,

406
00:36:14,200 --> 00:36:16,120
we don't pay for problems that you knew you had

407
00:36:16,120 --> 00:36:18,320
when you bought the insurance.

408
00:36:18,320 --> 00:36:19,840
Now, when you think about it, that's

409
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:23,920
almost like banning the prevention of insurance fraud.

410
00:36:23,920 --> 00:36:24,420
Right?

411
00:36:24,420 --> 00:36:26,200
Because this is saying that it's like you.

412
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:29,720
Well, you can't refuse to sell insurance to people

413
00:36:29,720 --> 00:36:33,320
just because they're already mortally ill.

414
00:36:33,320 --> 00:36:36,120
So this is a regulation that has something

415
00:36:36,120 --> 00:36:37,320
to do with adverse selection.

416
00:36:37,320 --> 00:36:40,160
Although, by itself, what is the connection

417
00:36:40,160 --> 00:36:41,600
between this and adverse selection?

418
00:36:41,600 --> 00:36:42,200
Means to force.

419
00:36:42,200 --> 00:36:43,000
Yes.

420
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:44,480
So there's this part that actually

421
00:36:44,480 --> 00:36:45,760
is connected to the model.

422
00:36:45,760 --> 00:36:47,980
This part, where if you believe the model is actually

423
00:36:47,980 --> 00:36:49,960
intensifying the problem.

424
00:36:49,960 --> 00:36:55,040
And then finally, there's the third part,

425
00:36:55,040 --> 00:36:56,080
which also very popular.

426
00:36:56,080 --> 00:36:57,440
These are the subsidies.

427
00:36:57,440 --> 00:37:01,520
So this is the part where you say, well,

428
00:37:01,520 --> 00:37:03,200
once we mandate you have to have it,

429
00:37:03,200 --> 00:37:04,200
a lot of people can't afford it.

430
00:37:04,200 --> 00:37:05,620
So the subsidies are there to make

431
00:37:05,620 --> 00:37:07,360
sure everybody can afford it.

432
00:37:07,360 --> 00:37:11,120
And Krugman actually, very reasonably,

433
00:37:11,120 --> 00:37:16,320
goes and says that this is Obamacare here.

434
00:37:16,320 --> 00:37:18,720
And these are the three legs of Obamacare.

435
00:37:18,720 --> 00:37:20,560
It's a stool with three legs.

436
00:37:20,560 --> 00:37:23,640
And he needs all three legs to be stable.

437
00:37:23,640 --> 00:37:25,720
And the way that he explains it, well,

438
00:37:25,720 --> 00:37:29,720
once you ban pre-existing conditions clauses,

439
00:37:29,720 --> 00:37:32,440
and you can actually also think about this combined

440
00:37:32,440 --> 00:37:36,120
with people rates or pretty equal rates,

441
00:37:36,120 --> 00:37:39,400
not exactly equal, but think about this in general

442
00:37:39,400 --> 00:37:40,560
as rate regulation.

443
00:37:40,560 --> 00:37:43,160
So once you go and say that any person can get insurance,

444
00:37:43,160 --> 00:37:44,960
no matter what the state of their health is,

445
00:37:44,960 --> 00:37:47,000
and that you're not allowed to go and charge them

446
00:37:47,000 --> 00:37:49,240
much more money, then the obvious thing

447
00:37:49,240 --> 00:37:51,000
in terms of the adverse selection model,

448
00:37:51,000 --> 00:37:52,040
how will people respond?

449
00:37:54,560 --> 00:37:56,760
You can get insurance at the normal rate

450
00:37:56,760 --> 00:37:59,320
if you just wait around until you really, really need it.

451
00:37:59,320 --> 00:38:01,720
So adverse selection says that people will do what?

452
00:38:01,720 --> 00:38:02,220
Wait around.

453
00:38:02,220 --> 00:38:03,920
Wait around.

454
00:38:03,920 --> 00:38:09,360
So if you wait around, then the problem is that the market

455
00:38:09,360 --> 00:38:11,320
equilibrium will be certain high prices.

456
00:38:11,320 --> 00:38:13,360
So that's where Krugman says, well,

457
00:38:13,360 --> 00:38:15,480
once we've done this regulation, then we

458
00:38:15,480 --> 00:38:17,320
got to do that regulation to go and solve

459
00:38:17,320 --> 00:38:19,280
the problems created by the first regulation.

460
00:38:19,280 --> 00:38:21,860
And then we need this regulation to solve the problems created

461
00:38:21,860 --> 00:38:24,120
by the other regulation.

462
00:38:24,120 --> 00:38:27,280
And it is a reasonable package.

463
00:38:27,280 --> 00:38:29,160
So I like the case for it.

464
00:38:29,160 --> 00:38:30,720
It's a very clear case.

465
00:38:30,720 --> 00:38:33,840
Although to go and say that Obamacare is there

466
00:38:33,840 --> 00:38:38,000
because the free market doesn't work is a gross injustice.

467
00:38:38,000 --> 00:38:39,720
Because he said, well, how about we just

468
00:38:39,720 --> 00:38:42,760
have none of these regulations?

469
00:38:42,760 --> 00:38:45,280
Then would there still be a problem?

470
00:38:45,280 --> 00:38:48,360
In particular, there's something really weird about this one.

471
00:38:48,360 --> 00:38:50,800
Because if you believe that insurance companies couldn't

472
00:38:50,800 --> 00:38:53,640
figure out who was sick and who was healthy,

473
00:38:53,640 --> 00:38:56,480
would there be any point of that regulation?

474
00:38:56,480 --> 00:38:57,360
Yeah.

475
00:38:57,360 --> 00:38:58,720
Why do you have that regulation?

476
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:03,040
Because insurance companies do know.

477
00:39:03,040 --> 00:39:05,200
Insurance companies have armies of actuaries

478
00:39:05,200 --> 00:39:07,120
to do the run of statistics to figure out

479
00:39:07,120 --> 00:39:08,520
what your health status is.

480
00:39:08,520 --> 00:39:09,880
They've got lots of information.

481
00:39:09,880 --> 00:39:10,720
They can do tests.

482
00:39:10,720 --> 00:39:11,760
They can use your age.

483
00:39:11,760 --> 00:39:13,560
They can use past expenses.

484
00:39:13,560 --> 00:39:16,040
There's tons of stuff that they can do in order to figure it

485
00:39:16,040 --> 00:39:16,600
out.

486
00:39:16,600 --> 00:39:19,280
So this is the regulation that, at minimum,

487
00:39:19,280 --> 00:39:21,760
it makes the adverse selection problem worse.

488
00:39:21,760 --> 00:39:23,480
And then tries to, at first, makes it worse

489
00:39:23,480 --> 00:39:25,040
and then solves it another way.

490
00:39:25,040 --> 00:39:27,400
But it's also a kind of confession of guilt

491
00:39:27,400 --> 00:39:29,040
and saying, all right, fine.

492
00:39:29,040 --> 00:39:32,520
It's actually not the case that insurance companies don't

493
00:39:32,520 --> 00:39:33,400
know what's going on.

494
00:39:33,400 --> 00:39:34,680
Insurance companies know a lot.

495
00:39:34,680 --> 00:39:36,440
That's why they're going to go and charge sick people more

496
00:39:36,440 --> 00:39:39,080
rates, which would solve the problem we're actually

497
00:39:39,080 --> 00:39:40,560
talking about.

498
00:39:40,560 --> 00:39:43,840
But that's not our actual complaint.

499
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:46,080
And then this brings you back into the normal world

500
00:39:46,080 --> 00:39:47,800
that has nothing to do with economics.

501
00:39:47,800 --> 00:39:51,840
What does a normal person want to go and require

502
00:39:51,840 --> 00:39:54,880
either ban pre-existing conditions clauses

503
00:39:54,880 --> 00:39:56,800
and require equal rates?

504
00:39:56,800 --> 00:39:59,320
Normal, economically illiterate person.

505
00:39:59,320 --> 00:40:00,800
They find it unreasonable.


1
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:02,740
for a sick person to find it hard to wake up.

2
00:40:02,740 --> 00:40:03,740
Yeah, that's all it is.

3
00:40:03,740 --> 00:40:06,300
It's got nothing to do with any complex model.

4
00:40:06,300 --> 00:40:08,580
It's just like, why these mean insurance companies

5
00:40:08,580 --> 00:40:10,380
are going and charging high rates to people

6
00:40:10,380 --> 00:40:12,540
who really need health care.

7
00:40:12,540 --> 00:40:13,940
Then there is the puzzle, well, but why

8
00:40:13,940 --> 00:40:15,300
is it that people who sell insurance

9
00:40:15,300 --> 00:40:17,380
are the ones who have the responsibility for taking

10
00:40:17,380 --> 00:40:18,660
care of sick people?

11
00:40:18,660 --> 00:40:21,620
Why is it like, so if I'm going to go and deal with a problem,

12
00:40:21,620 --> 00:40:24,340
then it's up to me to go and solve the problem

13
00:40:24,340 --> 00:40:26,580
for free for other people?

14
00:40:26,580 --> 00:40:29,500
Again, it's one thing to say that there

15
00:40:29,500 --> 00:40:31,140
needs to be a government program or charity

16
00:40:31,140 --> 00:40:33,220
to solve it as to why you go and charge your anger

17
00:40:33,220 --> 00:40:34,980
at insurance companies.

18
00:40:34,980 --> 00:40:37,540
That is, again, pretty odd.

19
00:40:37,540 --> 00:40:41,780
So in the end, you say, well, what could you do instead?

20
00:40:41,780 --> 00:40:43,700
Well, you could have a government voucher

21
00:40:43,700 --> 00:40:46,460
program for sick people.

22
00:40:46,460 --> 00:40:47,420
You could do that.

23
00:40:47,420 --> 00:40:48,520
And then you say, well, isn't that

24
00:40:48,520 --> 00:40:50,680
just going to create this adverse selection problem?

25
00:40:50,680 --> 00:40:53,460
Well, how about if you don't give the voucher to people

26
00:40:53,460 --> 00:40:55,700
who waited to buy insurance?

27
00:40:55,700 --> 00:40:58,700
So if you actually already, if you've

28
00:40:58,700 --> 00:41:00,500
been buying insurance your whole life,

29
00:41:00,500 --> 00:41:01,900
and then you suddenly get really sick,

30
00:41:01,900 --> 00:41:03,060
and then nobody wants to sell you

31
00:41:03,060 --> 00:41:05,900
insurance at an affordable rate, then you get the voucher.

32
00:41:05,900 --> 00:41:07,940
Worse, if you're just going along your whole life

33
00:41:07,940 --> 00:41:10,380
refusing to buy, then say, well, sorry.

34
00:41:10,380 --> 00:41:11,260
That's your problem.

35
00:41:11,260 --> 00:41:12,780
You should have been responsible.

36
00:41:12,780 --> 00:41:13,940
So you could do that.

37
00:41:13,940 --> 00:41:16,820
Of course, also, you could have charities

38
00:41:16,820 --> 00:41:18,740
that go and try to take care of the problem.

39
00:41:18,740 --> 00:41:20,220
Although, once you start thinking about that way,

40
00:41:20,220 --> 00:41:21,980
then there's the obvious thought of, well,

41
00:41:21,980 --> 00:41:23,900
isn't there a better way to spend the money?

42
00:41:23,900 --> 00:41:25,660
Which there almost certainly is, actually,

43
00:41:25,660 --> 00:41:28,220
if you looked into effective altruism.

44
00:41:28,220 --> 00:41:30,340
Because health care is so expensive.

45
00:41:30,340 --> 00:41:32,300
It's a really expensive way to solve problems.

46
00:41:32,300 --> 00:41:33,820
Whereas, you could just go and do

47
00:41:33,820 --> 00:41:36,140
deworming pills in rural countries

48
00:41:36,140 --> 00:41:39,340
and help a lot more people that way.

49
00:41:39,340 --> 00:41:42,260
All right, so questions about this?

50
00:41:42,260 --> 00:41:43,740
Questions?

51
00:41:43,740 --> 00:41:44,820
All right.

52
00:41:44,820 --> 00:41:47,100
All right, so in any case, so economists rarely

53
00:41:47,100 --> 00:41:48,940
notice all the insurance regulations

54
00:41:48,940 --> 00:41:50,900
that are on the books that seem to be designed

55
00:41:50,900 --> 00:41:52,740
to make adverse selection worse.

56
00:41:52,740 --> 00:41:55,500
So there are lots of regulations that specifically

57
00:41:55,500 --> 00:41:59,860
forbid insurers from conditioning rates on risks.

58
00:41:59,860 --> 00:42:00,820
So that's one.

59
00:42:00,820 --> 00:42:02,660
And in terms of the adverse selection model,

60
00:42:02,660 --> 00:42:04,420
that makes no sense.

61
00:42:04,420 --> 00:42:06,780
Because if there's really this adverse selection problem,

62
00:42:06,780 --> 00:42:09,020
then insurance companies wouldn't be able to do it.

63
00:42:09,020 --> 00:42:10,460
Or if it's a more moderate problem,

64
00:42:10,460 --> 00:42:13,180
then at least they reduce the problem.

65
00:42:13,180 --> 00:42:14,700
So you might say, well, look, there's

66
00:42:14,700 --> 00:42:15,700
not perfect information.

67
00:42:15,700 --> 00:42:17,260
There's some adverse selection.

68
00:42:17,260 --> 00:42:19,100
There isn't any reason to prevent insurance companies

69
00:42:19,100 --> 00:42:21,740
from solving the problem as well as they are able.

70
00:42:21,740 --> 00:42:26,580
And at that point, say, now we'll see what can be done.

71
00:42:26,580 --> 00:42:29,580
Now, here's one that I have a lot of trouble understanding,

72
00:42:29,580 --> 00:42:31,860
even from a normal point of view.

73
00:42:31,860 --> 00:42:34,420
There are a lot of states that actually subsidize insurance

74
00:42:34,420 --> 00:42:36,740
for high-risk drivers.

75
00:42:36,740 --> 00:42:38,780
It's one thing to go and say, they're sick people,

