50
01:61:45,120 --> 01:61:46,520
Yeah, but not just that,

51
01:61:46,520 --> 01:61:49,200
it seems like a nursing major,

52
01:61:50,160 --> 01:61:53,000
I mean, yes, there's some costly signaling involved

53
01:61:53,000 --> 01:61:55,200
in one's engineering degree.

54
01:61:55,200 --> 01:61:56,760
There are actually a few times

55
01:61:56,760 --> 01:61:59,600
when you might use something in class in your real life.

56
01:61:59,600 --> 01:62:01,240
Yes, so the might is key,

57
01:62:01,240 --> 01:62:02,520
but here's the thing is,

58
01:62:02,520 --> 01:62:04,960
in general people often think that if a subject is hard

59
01:62:04,960 --> 01:62:05,960
and they don't know about it,

60
01:62:05,960 --> 01:62:07,480
that it's all human capital,

61
01:62:07,480 --> 01:62:09,160
and they think signaling is just important

62
01:62:09,160 --> 01:62:11,400
in all the fields they personally know,

63
01:62:11,400 --> 01:62:14,760
but I found that whenever I talk to anyone in any subject,

64
01:62:14,760 --> 01:62:16,480
no matter how technical it is,

65
01:62:16,480 --> 01:62:18,240
no matter how vocational it is,

66
01:62:18,240 --> 01:62:20,320
they always will say they had to jump through

67
01:62:20,320 --> 01:62:21,600
a bunch of meaningless hoops

68
01:62:21,600 --> 01:62:23,600
that were irrelevant to the job.

69
01:62:23,600 --> 01:62:24,800
I'm not saying that's not true,

70
01:62:24,800 --> 01:62:26,840
I'm saying, you know,

71
01:62:26,840 --> 01:62:30,440
if the meaningless hoops to the actual information

72
01:62:30,440 --> 01:62:35,440
in art history is maybe 99.99.01,

73
01:62:35,880 --> 01:62:38,000
in nursing, if you had 10% of useful information.

74
01:62:38,000 --> 01:62:40,480
Yeah, sure, totally believable,

75
01:62:40,480 --> 01:62:43,360
and I'd say my only reason for doubting is that,

76
01:62:43,360 --> 01:62:46,120
so engineering is a higher absolute return,

77
01:62:46,120 --> 01:62:48,480
so it could be the signaling percentage is lower,

78
01:62:48,480 --> 01:62:50,080
it could be the signaling percentage is the same,

79
01:62:50,080 --> 01:62:52,480
but the human capital part is just more valuable.

80
01:62:52,480 --> 01:62:55,520
Like when they are teaching skills,

81
01:62:55,520 --> 01:62:57,480
they're much more valuable skills.

82
01:62:57,480 --> 01:62:58,320
So I mean, I know that,

83
01:62:58,320 --> 01:63:00,320
so my dad's a PhD in electrical engineering,

84
01:63:00,320 --> 01:63:02,560
he said the main thing they did was proofs,

85
01:63:02,560 --> 01:63:05,560
which are never used on the job by a practicing engineer.

86
01:63:05,560 --> 01:63:06,520
I mean, not surprising,

87
01:63:06,520 --> 01:63:08,560
like who teaches engineering PhDs?

88
01:63:09,520 --> 01:63:11,520
Yes, professors of engineering,

89
01:63:11,520 --> 01:63:13,520
and the professors of engineering don't generally,

90
01:63:13,520 --> 01:63:14,800
they have some connection to industry,

91
01:63:14,800 --> 01:63:16,840
but most of them are just professors

92
01:63:16,840 --> 01:63:18,560
who are like other professors,

93
01:63:18,560 --> 01:63:20,240
they like talking about abstract subjects

94
01:63:20,240 --> 01:63:22,200
of no practical interest to anyone.

95
01:63:22,200 --> 01:63:23,960
My dad never stops telling the story

96
01:63:23,960 --> 01:63:26,120
about a seminar he attended in grad school

97
01:63:26,120 --> 01:63:28,400
about the design of a non-realizable engine.

98
01:63:29,280 --> 01:63:30,920
And my dad is a super practical guy,

99
01:63:30,920 --> 01:63:32,640
stupid, non-realizable engine,

100
01:63:32,640 --> 01:63:34,040
why in God are you figuring out

101
01:63:34,040 --> 01:63:36,000
how to design a non-realizable engine?

102
01:63:36,000 --> 01:63:38,240
And I'm saying, well, as a professor,

103
01:63:38,240 --> 01:63:39,280
that sounds really interesting,

104
01:63:39,280 --> 01:63:41,040
maybe there's some interesting lesson

105
01:63:41,040 --> 01:63:43,120
that you can learn by studying the non-realizable ones,

106
01:63:43,120 --> 01:63:45,280
and maybe it teaches you fundamental principles.

107
01:63:45,280 --> 01:63:46,360
Ah, bah!

108
01:63:46,360 --> 01:63:47,200
Right?

109
01:63:47,200 --> 01:63:48,040
Right?

110
01:63:48,040 --> 01:63:48,880
And it's all right, well fine,

111
01:63:48,880 --> 01:63:51,600
maybe it is of no use to actually designing any machine,

112
01:63:51,600 --> 01:63:55,200
but it's still important and intellectually interesting.

113
01:63:55,200 --> 01:63:56,040
Right?

114
01:63:56,040 --> 01:63:58,400
And that's not, in the business world,

115
01:63:58,400 --> 01:64:00,840
not considered very important.

116
01:64:00,840 --> 01:64:03,280
So I'm curious to know how widely accepted

117
01:64:03,280 --> 01:64:05,840
these sorts of concepts and this idea,

118
01:64:05,840 --> 01:64:07,280
especially the signaling idea,

119
01:64:07,280 --> 01:64:09,360
is being accepted, not just within economics,

120
01:64:09,360 --> 01:64:11,680
but within the education establishment,

121
01:64:11,680 --> 01:64:15,160
and education scholarship, and among education PhDs.

122
01:64:15,160 --> 01:64:16,200
And if they-

123
01:64:16,200 --> 01:64:18,280
Which idea, signaling or human capital?

124
01:64:18,280 --> 01:64:19,920
As far as the signaling idea,

125
01:64:19,920 --> 01:64:21,800
the fact that we're basically getting a certification

126
01:64:21,800 --> 01:64:23,680
or a license of some sort.

127
01:64:23,680 --> 01:64:25,320
Yeah, so what I'd say is,

128
01:64:25,320 --> 01:64:27,320
economists, strangely, are probably the most hostile

129
01:64:27,320 --> 01:64:28,440
to the signaling model.

130
01:64:28,440 --> 01:64:29,520
So not economists in general,

131
01:64:29,520 --> 01:64:32,480
but specialists in labor and education economics

132
01:64:32,480 --> 01:64:35,000
are generally very hostile to signaling.

133
01:64:35,000 --> 01:64:37,600
In fields like education, sociology, psychology,

134
01:64:37,600 --> 01:64:39,200
they're much more open to it.

135
01:64:39,200 --> 01:64:40,600
Although what they're not open to

136
01:64:40,600 --> 01:64:42,320
is drawing the inference that then maybe

137
01:64:42,320 --> 01:64:43,240
we don't want to give everyone

138
01:64:43,240 --> 01:64:45,040
the best education in the world.

139
01:64:45,040 --> 01:64:48,080
Maybe we've actually created a horrible prisoner, Selena,

140
01:64:48,080 --> 01:64:49,640
and we should cut back.

141
01:64:49,640 --> 01:64:51,480
So that's sort of the policy implication

142
01:64:51,480 --> 01:64:53,960
is very hard for non-economists to accept.

143
01:64:53,960 --> 01:64:55,240
Economists are really quick to say,

144
01:64:55,240 --> 01:64:56,400
well, sure, if we're signaling,

145
01:64:56,400 --> 01:64:57,960
then yeah, we're really,

146
01:64:57,960 --> 01:64:59,840
well, government is going and pouring fuel

147
01:64:59,840 --> 01:65:01,000
in the fire of waste.

148
01:65:02,600 --> 01:65:05,600
But then most economists can see backwards induction

149
01:65:05,600 --> 01:65:08,440
and realize they'd have to embrace a very personally

150
01:65:08,440 --> 01:65:10,520
and politically unpalatable conclusion.

151
01:65:10,520 --> 01:65:12,920
And so then they, at least my uncharitable theory,

152
01:65:12,920 --> 01:65:13,840
is they then say, oh my God,

153
01:65:13,840 --> 01:65:15,800
well, we can't accept a theory

154
01:65:15,800 --> 01:65:18,640
that would logically imply something so horrifying.

155
01:65:18,640 --> 01:65:20,040
So there's that.

156
01:65:20,040 --> 01:65:21,320
And also based on what you were saying

157
01:65:21,320 --> 01:65:25,200
about how little is retained from one year to the next,

158
01:65:25,200 --> 01:65:27,200
when you're looking at the growth literature

159
01:65:27,200 --> 01:65:28,960
and with comparisons of countries

160
01:65:28,960 --> 01:65:31,520
that have X number of years of education

161
01:65:31,520 --> 01:65:32,720
and schooling and whatnot,

162
01:65:32,720 --> 01:65:35,320
and I'm sure you might be doing that here in the US,

163
01:65:36,240 --> 01:65:38,120
how applicable is that really

164
01:65:38,120 --> 01:65:41,560
if we're talking about the US, the number one economy,

165
01:65:41,560 --> 01:65:45,640
where you have 20, mid-20 somethings,

166
01:65:45,640 --> 01:65:48,000
you don't know what the three branches of government is,

167
01:65:48,000 --> 01:65:52,200
but they've had 12, 16 plus years of schooling.

168
01:65:55,000 --> 01:65:56,400
Can we say that that's really robust

169
01:65:56,400 --> 01:65:57,240
or is that gonna start?

170
01:65:57,240 --> 01:66:00,360
Yeah, well, I mean, if you're a diehard human capital fan,

171
01:66:00,360 --> 01:66:01,960
you could always just say, look,

172
01:66:01,960 --> 01:66:03,120
it's really disappointing

173
01:66:03,120 --> 01:66:05,560
that education doesn't improve people more,

174
01:66:05,560 --> 01:66:09,160
but the important thing is that it improves you enough

175
01:66:09,160 --> 01:66:10,200
to justify higher pay.

176
01:66:10,200 --> 01:66:13,320
So saying, look, someone who is half literate,

177
01:66:14,520 --> 01:66:15,680
well, not half literate,

178
01:66:15,680 --> 01:66:18,640
but someone who can read okay is much more employable

179
01:66:18,640 --> 01:66:20,720
than someone who can't read at all.

180
01:66:20,720 --> 01:66:22,520
Right, and then that makes sense.

181
01:66:22,520 --> 01:66:24,920
And so you might very well be willing to pay 30% more

182
01:66:24,920 --> 01:66:27,680
for someone who can read poorly

183
01:66:27,680 --> 01:66:29,680
than for someone who can't read at all.

184
01:66:29,680 --> 01:66:33,600
But then again, there's the puzzle

185
01:66:33,600 --> 01:66:34,840
of what about all these other subjects

186
01:66:34,840 --> 01:66:37,320
that people get penalized for not knowing?

187
01:66:37,320 --> 01:66:38,680
So, so, seems in effect to me

188
01:66:38,680 --> 01:66:40,160
is one of the most compelling arguments

189
01:66:40,160 --> 01:66:41,520
for the power of signaling.

190
01:66:42,880 --> 01:66:44,040
Another one, just as you're alluding to,

191
01:66:44,040 --> 01:66:46,400
so there are efforts to go and compare

192
01:66:46,400 --> 01:66:48,240
the effect of education on individuals

193
01:66:48,240 --> 01:66:50,200
versus the effect on countries.

194
01:66:50,200 --> 01:66:51,760
And the big punchline out of this literature

195
01:66:51,760 --> 01:66:54,760
is that if you raise an individual's income,

196
01:66:54,760 --> 01:66:57,560
by race, if you raise an individual's education by a year,

197
01:66:57,560 --> 01:66:59,880
income goes up by maybe 10%.

198
01:66:59,880 --> 01:67:02,640
If you raise a country's income by,

199
01:67:02,640 --> 01:67:04,620
the average income in a country by a year

200
01:67:04,620 --> 01:67:07,840
only seems to go up by about 2%.

201
01:67:07,840 --> 01:67:10,800
So in other words, the effect of education

202
01:67:10,800 --> 01:67:13,360
on individual income seems to be about five times as large

203
01:67:13,360 --> 01:67:16,320
as for national income, which makes perfect sense here.

204
01:67:16,320 --> 01:67:20,040
It's almost all of it signaling

205
01:67:20,040 --> 01:67:22,040
because if one person gets more education,

206
01:67:22,040 --> 01:67:23,680
you look better compared to the other people.

207
01:67:23,680 --> 01:67:25,080
But if everyone gets more education,

208
01:67:25,080 --> 01:67:27,600
you just need more education to look good.

209
01:67:27,600 --> 01:67:29,440
Now, a reply to this that some people have made

210
01:67:29,440 --> 01:67:31,840
is well, look, here's the real problem.

211
01:67:31,840 --> 01:67:33,200
Education in a lot of these,

212
01:67:33,200 --> 01:67:34,640
a lot of verbal countries is terrible.

213
01:67:34,640 --> 01:67:36,240
The teachers don't even show up.

214
01:67:36,240 --> 01:67:39,480
So of course, it isn't really helping their growth very much.

215
01:67:39,480 --> 01:67:42,200
But that would predict that in countries like India

216
01:67:42,200 --> 01:67:44,400
that have these horrible education systems,

217
01:67:44,400 --> 01:67:47,320
that the private return to education would also be low.

218
01:67:47,320 --> 01:67:49,040
It's not.

219
01:67:49,040 --> 01:67:51,040
So in India, where the teachers aren't even showing up,

220
01:67:51,040 --> 01:67:52,880
it still looks like an extra year of education

221
01:67:52,880 --> 01:67:56,040
is buying you an extra 10% of wages for the rest of your life.

222
01:67:57,240 --> 01:68:00,440
But the national effect seems to be very small.

223
01:68:00,440 --> 01:68:02,880
Another set of evidence that I really like

224
01:68:02,880 --> 01:68:04,320
is there's a whole literature,

225
01:68:04,320 --> 01:68:06,040
more in sociology than economics,

226
01:68:06,040 --> 01:68:07,620
although some economists can do it,

227
01:68:07,620 --> 01:68:12,620
on what's called malemployment,

228
01:68:14,080 --> 01:68:16,080
or sometimes called overeducation,

229
01:68:16,080 --> 01:68:17,920
or just called credential inflation.

230
01:68:22,560 --> 01:68:24,880
And it's the idea that over time,

231
01:68:24,880 --> 01:68:27,080
the educational credentials that you need to get

232
01:68:27,080 --> 01:68:28,960
a given job have gone up,

233
01:68:28,960 --> 01:68:30,720
even though the jobs themselves

234
01:68:30,720 --> 01:68:33,020
are no more complicated than they were before.

235
01:68:34,120 --> 01:68:35,880
All right, so you can look at this

236
01:68:35,880 --> 01:68:37,760
and see that now it is totally common

237
01:68:37,760 --> 01:68:40,200
for waiters to have college degrees.

238
01:68:40,200 --> 01:68:42,760
All right, so what's going on?

239
01:68:42,760 --> 01:68:43,920
Is it, well, it's the fancy restaurants,

240
01:68:43,920 --> 01:68:45,360
they wanna have waiters with college degrees

241
01:68:45,360 --> 01:68:48,600
who go and deal better with the customers.

242
01:68:48,600 --> 01:68:50,320
Do you need four years for that?

243
01:68:51,440 --> 01:68:52,840
Like, why couldn't you just do, like,

244
01:68:52,840 --> 01:68:55,080
one year of waiter school or something like that,

245
01:68:55,080 --> 01:68:57,040
if it's all about just being able to go

246
01:68:57,040 --> 01:68:59,520
and mingle with high-class customers?

247
01:68:59,520 --> 01:69:01,120
I mean, it seems much more like

248
01:69:01,120 --> 01:69:04,560
now that we have so many people with college degrees,

249
01:69:04,560 --> 01:69:06,400
some of them lined up not being able

250
01:69:06,400 --> 01:69:07,800
to get a regular college-type job,

251
01:69:07,800 --> 01:69:09,240
and so they became waiters.

252
01:69:10,280 --> 01:69:12,840
Right, so there's a little literature on this.

253
01:69:12,840 --> 01:69:16,040
Oh, yeah, and bottom line is that

254
01:69:16,040 --> 01:69:17,880
when people have tried to measure,

255
01:69:17,880 --> 01:69:21,560
or disaggregate how much of the rise in education

256
01:69:21,560 --> 01:69:24,680
is explained by changing of the jobs that we do

257
01:69:24,680 --> 01:69:27,000
into more cognitively demanding jobs,

258
01:69:27,000 --> 01:69:29,320
and how much of it can be explained

259
01:69:29,320 --> 01:69:32,400
or accounted by rising education requirements

260
01:69:32,400 --> 01:69:35,360
within given jobs, the rough breakdown looks like

261
01:69:35,360 --> 01:69:38,360
about 20% of the education rise accounted for

262
01:69:38,360 --> 01:69:40,440
with more cognitively demanding jobs,

263
01:69:40,440 --> 01:69:44,680
80% the giant remainder just that you now need

264
01:69:44,680 --> 01:69:46,400
more education to get a given job.

265
01:69:47,480 --> 01:69:48,600
You might need a high school degree

266
01:69:48,600 --> 01:69:50,280
to be a garbage man now.

267
01:69:50,280 --> 01:69:51,200
Things like this.

268
01:69:51,200 --> 01:69:52,440
Now, of course, sometimes you may say,

269
01:69:52,440 --> 01:69:53,840
well, maybe it's for government jobs

270
01:69:53,840 --> 01:69:55,360
or jobs where there's an education requirement

271
01:69:55,360 --> 01:69:56,680
for licensing.

272
01:69:56,680 --> 01:69:58,000
That may be a little bit of it,

273
01:69:58,000 --> 01:70:01,200
but we can actually go and look at all of the industries,

274
01:70:01,200 --> 01:70:04,080
like private sector employment or unlicensed industries,

275
01:70:04,080 --> 01:70:06,120
and we can still see, even there,

276
01:70:06,120 --> 01:70:09,960
it looks like employers are paying substantial premiums

277
01:70:09,960 --> 01:70:11,920
to go and get people with high school degrees

278
01:70:11,920 --> 01:70:15,000
or college degrees for jobs that common sense says

279
01:70:15,000 --> 01:70:17,360
are not college jobs,

280
01:70:17,360 --> 01:70:19,400
they're not even really high school jobs.

281
01:70:20,680 --> 01:70:23,000
And then there's one other literature,

282
01:70:23,000 --> 01:70:24,640
but it's a little complicated, so I won't go into it,

283
01:70:24,640 --> 01:70:26,320
but I'll leave something for the imagination

284
01:70:26,320 --> 01:70:29,320
in case you wanna take the class.

285
01:70:29,320 --> 01:70:32,280
All right, so bottom line is

286
01:70:32,280 --> 01:70:33,360
the signaling model for education

287
01:70:33,360 --> 01:70:34,200
seems to make a lot of sense.

288
01:70:34,200 --> 01:70:35,040
Oh yeah, one other thing.

289
01:70:35,040 --> 01:70:37,040
So I also have a bunch of appeals

290
01:70:37,040 --> 01:70:38,800
to firsthand experience that I like.

291
01:70:38,800 --> 01:70:40,200
So I've already kind of done one,

292
01:70:40,200 --> 01:70:43,120
which is why is it that you worry about

293
01:70:44,240 --> 01:70:46,040
failing a class, but you don't worry about

294
01:70:46,040 --> 01:70:48,800
forgetting the material in the class after the final exam?

295
01:70:49,680 --> 01:70:51,920
In human capital terms, that's pretty odd,

296
01:70:51,920 --> 01:70:54,280
because from the point of view of employers,

297
01:70:54,280 --> 01:70:55,520
there's no difference between someone

298
01:70:55,520 --> 01:70:58,280
who forgot the material and someone who never knew it.

299
01:70:58,280 --> 01:71:01,400
But in signaling terms, there's a big difference,

300
01:71:01,400 --> 01:71:04,560
because if you did a class that you didn't really like,

301
01:71:04,560 --> 01:71:07,040
got an A, and then forgot all the material,

302
01:71:07,040 --> 01:71:09,440
you still prove something about yourself.

303
01:71:09,440 --> 01:71:11,480
And as long as willingness to submit

304
01:71:11,480 --> 01:71:15,280
to boring, pointless tasks is persistent throughout life,

305
01:71:15,280 --> 01:71:16,880
the fact that this one person went

306
01:71:16,880 --> 01:71:18,400
and did a bunch of these things before

307
01:71:18,400 --> 01:71:19,680
and then forgot the material

308
01:71:19,680 --> 01:71:21,000
still makes them more employable

309
01:71:21,000 --> 01:71:21,840
than someone who just said,

310
01:71:21,840 --> 01:71:24,680
"'Screw you, I don't wanna do that stuff, it's stupid.'"

311
01:71:24,680 --> 01:71:25,520
Yeah?

312
01:71:25,520 --> 01:71:28,360
If you've learned something once and you forget it,

313
01:71:28,360 --> 01:71:29,360
you can learn it faster.

314
01:71:29,360 --> 01:71:31,640
That's very true, and there's plenty of literature on that,

315
01:71:31,640 --> 01:71:34,040
but what if you don't even use it on the job?

316
01:71:34,040 --> 01:71:36,800
Yeah, so why is it that you don't worry about that?

317
